In Java multi-threading is concurrent execution of 2 or more threads to maximize the utilization of the CPU.
Multithreading is in java.lang package

In SINGLE-CORE environment, Java's multithreading is managed by the JVM and the OS, which switch between thread to give illusion of concurrency.
The thread share the single core and time-slicing is used to manage thread execution.

In MULTI_CORE environment, Java's multithreading can take full advantage the available cors.
The JVM can distribute threads across multiple cores, allowing true parallel execution of threads.

A thread is a lightweight process, the smallest unit of the processing. Java supports multithreading through its java.lang.Thread class and java.lang.Runnable interface.

When java program starts, one thread begins running immediately, which is called the main thread.
This thread is responsible for executing the main method of the program.

To create a new thread in Java u can either extend "Thread" or implement "Runnable" interface.

STEPS USING Thread class ->
        1. Create a class that extends Thread.
        2. Override the run method of the class.
        3. Create the object of the class.
        3. start() method is called to initiate the new thread.

STEPS USING Runnable interface ->
        1. Create the class that implements Runnable.
        2. Override the run method.
        3. Create the object the class.
        4. Create an object of the Thread and pass the instance of the class to the Thread constructor.
        5. call the strat() method from the instance of the Thread object.